    "chunk_text": "\u0dda \u0db0\u0dcf\u0db8\u0db1 \u0dad \u0db8\u0dd6\u0dbd\u200d \u0db4\u0dca\u200d\u0dbb\u0dad\u0dd2\u0db4\u0dca \u0dad\u0dd2 \u0d89\u0dbd\u0d9a\u0dca\u0d9a \u0db4\u0dd4\u0dbb\u0dcf \u0d9c\u0dd0\u0db1\u0dd3\u0db8 \u0dc1\u0dca\u200d\u0dbb\u0dd3 \u0dbd\u0dcf\u0d82\u0d9a\u0dcf \u0db8\u0dad \u0db6\u0dd0\u0dcf\u0d82\u0d9a\u0dd4\u0dc0 \u0d85\u0dc0\u0db4\u0dcf\u0dc0\u0dad\u0dcf \u0dca \u0dc0\u0dd3\u0db8 \u0b87\u0bb2\u0b99\u0bcd\u0bb2\u0b95 \u0bc8\u0ba4\u0bcd\u0ba4\u0bbf\u0baf \u0bc6\u0b99\u0bcd\u0b95\u0bbf\u0baf\u0bc6\u0bbe\u0bb2\u0bcd \u0baa\u0ba3\u0bb5\u0bc0\u0b95\u0bcd\u0b95 \u0bc8\u0bb1\u0bcd\u0bb1\u0bc1\u0bae\u0bcd \u0ba8\u0bbf\u0ba4\u0bbf\u0b95\u0bcd\n\n\u0ba4\u0b95\u0bc6\u0bbe\u0bb3\u0bcd\u0bb2\u0b95 \u0b87\u0bb2\u0b95\u0bcd\u0b95\u0bc1\u0b95\u0bb2\u0bb3 \u0b85\u0bb2\u0b9f\u0baf \u0bae\u0bc1\u0b9f\u0bbf\u0baf\u0bc6\u0bbe\u0bb2\u0bc8 FAILURE TO MEET INFLATION AND MONETARY POLICY\n\nTARGETS BY CENTRAL BANK OF SRI LANKA\n\nFollowing the enactment of the new Central Bank of Sri Lanka Act, the Central Bank was granted enhanced independence in the formulation and execution of the monetary policy. However, such independence must be matched by a clearly defined framework of accountability. In this context, the critical question arises as to whom the Central Bank is now accountable and through what institutional or public mechanism that accountability is exercised. Sir, I quote from Section 5(2) of the Central Bank of Sri Lanka Act, No. 16 of 2023.\n\n\"The Central Bank shall be autonomous and accountable.\"\n\nSir, their autonomy is there, but their accountability is nowhere to be seen. Time and again, the Central Bank of Sri Lanka has attributed its failure to meet inflation targets to fiscal slippage, external shocks and supply-side constraints. However, such explanations cannot substitute for timely and effective monetary action in the context of full independence and the freedom for monetary policy-making. Notably, in the face of renewed inflationary pressures in recent quarters, the Central Bank of Sri Lanka refrained from adjusting policy rates in a timely manner, undermining its credibility and ability to anchor inflation expectations. Over the years, since its inception in 1950, the Central Bank of Sri Lank has beaten fiscal authorities for maintaining a higher Budget deficit, that being the scapegoat goat for covering up of its monetary policy failures. The Central Bank of Sri Lanka cannot do it any more since the 15th of September 2023, the day on which the new Central Bank of Sri Lanka Act, No. 16 of 2023 became effective. The Central Bank of Sri Lanka is now fully independent and free to make its own policies."
