    "md5": "2c818a123b3a03089153f273c40776a9",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1673,
    "chunk_text": "While I respect to the Answer you gave, Hon. Deputy Minister, I must state that it does not fully anwer the Question I raised to ensure that you understand the gravity of the accountability of the Central Bank. Basically, the Central Bank's appearing twice before the Committee on Public Finance and the COPE does not demonstrate its accountability. As for the Governor and the Central Bank, it is not clear to whom they are directly responsible. There is a necessity for that when it comes to an institution of this nature. Take, for instance, a citizen who wants to buy a car, a house, invest overseas or seek priority sector lending. Even government salaries are approved by the Central Bank, but the Governor\u2019s own salary is approved by himself.\n\nSo, Sir, my point is that this country\u2019s economy has gone through too many turbulent times and it is necessary to bring about a sense of accountability and to make this institution report directly to someone. Now that you all have got a two-thirds majority, if there are half-baked legislations, it is time to have them corrected. I am saying with empirical evidence and with practical experience.\n\nThis country cannot go through another 22nd May type operation. Therefore, my question to you, Hon. Deputy Minister, is, if there is a sense of independence for the Central Bank, what was the he doing in Washington when he has a lot to do with negotiations regarding Donald Trump's tariff? This is a breach of that sense of independence, Hon. Deputy Minister. So, I would appreciate if you, with your knowledge, would take this on, Hon. Deputy Minister, and ensure that corrective action is taken with immediate effect."
  },
