    "md5": "730dd47c00c963f0208cf80fc25d8334",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1971,
    "chunk_text": "He studied at St. Mary\u2019s Convent, Trincomalee, St. Joseph\u2019s Tamil College, Trincomalee, St. Anne\u2019s College, Kurunegala, St. Sebastian\u2019s College, Moratuwa and St. Patrick\u2019s College, Jaffna and then, entered the Ceylon Law College in 1951 and became a lawyer.\n\n1141 1142\n\n\u0daf\u0dcf\u0dca\u0dd2\u0dba\u0dca\u0db1\u0dca\u0dad\u0dd4\u0db8\u0dc0\n\nIn 2001, he assumed leadership of the Tamil National Alliance - TNA - a coalition of Tamil parties. He remained at the helm of the TNA until his demise, providing steady and principled leadership - I reiterate, Sir, providing steady and principled leadership - to that Tamil political movement.\n\nThe Hon. Sampanthan was also the senior-most Member, having represented Parliament from 1977 to 1983 - we know why he left Parliament in 1983 - then from 1997 to 2000 and from 2001 onwards until his death. He served as the 14th Leader of the Opposition in Parliament from 2015 to 2018, becoming the second Tamil Leader of the Opposition in Parliament, 38 years after the Hon. Appapillai Amirthalingam became the Leader of the Opposition in 1977.\n\nHe was widely recognized as a national and a statesmanlike figure within Sri Lankan politics. A tireless advocate for peace, reconciliation and justice, the Hon. Sampanthan played a central and a stabilizing role in Tamil political discourse. His political ideology was deeply rooted in the aspiration to harmonize the rights of all ethnic groups, with a special concern on the Tamil people, within a united Sri Lanka. Over six decades, he earned the reputation of a principled patriot, one who pursued national unity through dialogue and understanding.\n\nA principled politician who unwaveringly championed social justice, he earned deep respect, both locally and internationally. He consistently advocated for peace and the rights of the Sri Lankan people, often representing their cause on numerous official visits abroad.\n\nHe was married to Mrs. Leeladevi and was a loving father of two sons and a daughter."
  },
