    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "02ac4113ecfd0345893175af684ac3a5",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1970,
    "chunk_text": "The late Hon. Sampanthan served this House for over half a century across multiple chapters of Sri Lanka\u2019s post-Independence history. His Parliamentary career was not just long, but was principled. It was shaped by an unwavering commitment to justice, democracy and most of all, the dignity and the political rights of the Tamil people. He believed in meaningful power-sharing, reconciliation without erasure, accountability and in a just and an honourable solution to the national question. He sought peace, not surrender. He spoke gently, but the truth he carried thundered through the halls and beyond this Parliament. What set the Hon. Sampanthan apart was not just his intellect or experience, but his moral clarity. He refused to abandon our cause even when it was politically inconvenient or personally costly and through it all, he conducted himself with a grace that earned the respect of both the allies and adversaries. He believed in dialogue, but never at the cost of principle. He carried forward the mandate of a wounded and often silenced community and carried it with immense grace and discipline.\n\nAnother significant moment in his career was during his tenure as the Leader of the Opposition. When President Maithreepala Sirisena, during the coup, appointed Mahinda Rajapaksa as the Leader of the Opposition of Sri Lanka, the Hon. Sampanthan took the responsibility upon himself to uphold democracy and went to the Supreme Court. I think he was the petitioner in that case, which later led President Maithreepala Sirisena to reverse his decision. So, the late Hon. Sampanthan\u2019s contribution has not been just for the Tamil community, but for the entire country.\n\nSo, in remembering the Hon. Sampanthan, to this House I would say, let us reflect on what he stood for: meaningful reconciliation, equal rights and a country where no community must beg for dignity. That is the unfinished work of this Parliament; that is the duty we inherit from him."
  },
