    "date_str": "2025-08-06",
    "description": "Hansard of August 06, 2025",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.parliament.lk/en/business-of-parliament/hansards",
    "lang": "si-ta-en",
    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/documents/hansard/1755159820030645.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2025-08-06-2025-08-06-0157",
    "chunk_index": 157,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "8c1918d47f9dd39039b3848c09eb2f08",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1911,
    "chunk_text": "The CEB\u2019s losses and infrastructure needs are fiscally unsustainable, all of us know that. Our ability to maintain the country\u2019s primary surplus and meet the International Monetary Fund commitments is well known. With that issue, we are going to further undermine the country\u2019s creditworthiness, limiting access to capital markets and affordable borrowing.\n\nSir, now, I would like to read from an article in the \"Daily FT\" titled \u201cSri Lanka\u2019s Electricity Amendment Bill 2025: Trojan Horse for national economic collapse\u201d. This is what it states, I quote:\n\n\u201cThe National People\u2019s Power Government rose to power promising transparency, institutional reform and a green energy revolution. The President vowed to build an accountable electricity regime; the Energy Minister committed to regulator independence and market modernisation. Yet, the Electricity Amendment Bill 2025 delivers the opposite. It entrenches ministerial control, resurrects monopolies, enables corruption, and locks the nation into decades of coal dependence. Enacting this Bill will devastate donor confidence, repel investors, deepen energy poverty and reverse Sri Lanka\u2019s hard-earned progress.\u201d\n\nSo, that is what this particular article states. There are some details in it, but I do not want to go into those details.\n\nThen, I will move on to some other issue which is now being discussed in the energy circles. That is regarding the 2025 tariff revision for Standard Power Purchasing Agreements governing projects up to 10 megawatts. Though this revision is presented as a routine, cost-reflective recalibration cloaked with references to the International Monetary Fund and its conditionality, the substance reveals a different story. This is not a correction; this is sabotage, a systemic, deliberate distortion by a group with vested interests that threatens to derail the Government\u2019s own renewable energy goals."
  },
  {
