    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "a88225c7ee9ff1afc171578ae387824d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1974,
    "chunk_text": "The people of this country gave this Government a clear mandate to reform the broken systems, dismantle inefficiencies, end corruption and to deliver affordable and reliable services to all. As articulated in the NPP Energy Policy, our energy transition by restructuring one monolithic organization such as the CEB would increase efficiency and transparency, inviting innovation and putting the people at the centre of the energy system.\n\nIntroduction of competitive bidding to build power plants and bulk purchase of electricity by NSO is essential in driving down costs and improving service quality. Sri Lanka is not alone in this journey. Many countries which started with a monolithic government organization for electricity, that is to handle from generation to distribution, like the CEB have successfully navigated in this path of restructuring. For example, Australia introduced real-time electricity bidding and day-ahead power procurement through its National Electricity Market - NEM. That has led to lower prices and improved reliability. Through Tenaga Nasional Berhad -TNB, Malaysia unbundled its operations while retaining government control in transmission. Independent Power Producers now generate a significant portion of electricity under competitive frameworks in\n\n1597 1598\n\n\u0db4\u0dcf\u0dbb\u0dca\u0dd2\u0dc1\u0db8\u0dca\u0db1\u0dca\u0dad\u0dd4\u0db81\n\nthat country. India also reformed its State-owned electricity boards into separate generation, transmission and distribution entities. Singapore operates its electricity sector under the Energy Market Authority - EMA - allowing consumers to choose among multiple electricity retailers. The market features real-time wholesale trading, smart grid integration and strict regulatory oversight to ensure competition, reliability and low system losses. South Africa, too, currently undergoes unbundling of Eskom into three separate entities for generation, transmission and distribution to overcome chronic inefficiencies and load shedding."
  },
