    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "8127ef19e182eb258d482fe73246dfa3",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1814,
    "chunk_text": "that country. India also reformed its State-owned electricity boards into separate generation, transmission and distribution entities. Singapore operates its electricity sector under the Energy Market Authority - EMA - allowing consumers to choose among multiple electricity retailers. The market features real-time wholesale trading, smart grid integration and strict regulatory oversight to ensure competition, reliability and low system losses. South Africa, too, currently undergoes unbundling of Eskom into three separate entities for generation, transmission and distribution to overcome chronic inefficiencies and load shedding.\n\nSir, one main question asked by the Opposition and the public is, will this restructuring reduce the tariff? The short answer is, yes, but with a realistic and phased approach. So, immediate cost reductions will come from introducing competition in power procurement, phasing out costly emergency power purchases, reducing technical and commercial losses and leveraging digital efficiencies. The other question asked is whether there will be an issue for the current workforce. This reform is not for dismantling of the workforce, but for transformation of the institution. The proposed Amendments include a 100 per cent government-owned employees' fund to secure pensions and welfare. Then, there will be employment guarantee for the employees in this transition to the new institutions under this new restructuring or the excess employees could go on voluntarily retirement through a VRS, safeguarding the current staff and their rights. Also, this restructuring involves training and upskilling of the current CEB employees to take on the future-ready roles. Then, transparent communication and HR restructuring to prevent uncertainty and fear are also included in the plan."
