    "md5": "ae3f9d9c2b6958366f93421989e07441",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1754,
    "chunk_text": "That is because of the industrial policies and strategies they conducted and we also had a continuingly increasing fiscal deficit, which led us to a lot of debts and the crisis we have been facing for the last few years. And, we, as a country, have the highest interest cost in the world compared to government revenue and has one of the highest number of people who cannot repay at these interest rates. So, these are the indicators we have been left with over the past few years and we were among the lowest tax collecting nations. The tax collection as a percentage of GDP was 8.5 per cent in 2019 and even now, we use indirect taxes to collect these revenues.\n\nSir, if you want to really look at the fundamentals needed for a country\u2019s economic growth, we need to take lessons from countries like Singapore. I was lucky enough to meet Dr. Kishore Mahbubani last week and to share some thoughts. He is famous in the economic sphere and says that the rule of law, meritocracy and discipline are the base for a country\u2019s economic growth. And, what we are trying to showcase is that this Government is following that path and those traits. We really need to ask the Opposition, \u201cHave you ever been able to exercise meritocracy?\u201d If you really look at their advisers to the Government and the CEOs of the State-owned institutions, were they of that calibre who could run the institutions with no interest in or with no affiliation to politics?\n\nAs you can see, the Hon. (Dr.) Anil Jayantha is leading the front as the Deputy Finance Minister and (Dr.) Harshana Suriyapperuma has been doing his part,\n\n1765 1766\n\n[\u0d9c\u0dbb\u0dd4 \u0d9a\u0db1 \u0dbb\u0dba\u0dda\u0dbd\u0dd4 \u0dc2\u0db1\u0dca\u0db8\u0dd4\u0d9c\u0db8\u0dca \u0d9a\u0dd4\u0d9c\u0daf\u0dca\u200d\u0dba\u0dcf\u0dc3\u0db1\u0dca \u0db8\u0dc4 \u0dcf]\n\n2025 \u0d85\u0dba\u0d9c\u0ddd\u0dc3\u0dc0\u0dc0 07"
  },
