    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "e8a2073b0a34c657cae6ab604232005c",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1646,
    "chunk_text": "As you can see, the Hon. (Dr.) Anil Jayantha is leading the front as the Deputy Finance Minister and (Dr.) Harshana Suriyapperuma has been doing his part,\n\n1765 1766\n\n[\u0d9c\u0dbb\u0dd4 \u0d9a\u0db1 \u0dbb\u0dba\u0dda\u0dbd\u0dd4 \u0dc2\u0db1\u0dca\u0db8\u0dd4\u0d9c\u0db8\u0dca \u0d9a\u0dd4\u0d9c\u0daf\u0dca\u200d\u0dba\u0dcf\u0dc3\u0db1\u0dca \u0db8\u0dc4 \u0dcf]\n\n2025 \u0d85\u0dba\u0d9c\u0ddd\u0dc3\u0dc0\u0dc0 07\n\nVery recently, I was fortunate to be part of a research finding forum conducted by Verit\u00e9 Research. At that forum, they explained how Sri Lanka is faring based on the assumptions of the Debt Sustainability Analysis of the IMF. It is very interesting to note that, as a country, we are doing far better than what was predicted for the country, and especially with regard to government revenue, we are doing better than what was expected with this year's revenue collection being 18 per cent more that that of the previous year. And the primary balance is better than what was expected and public debt, as a percentage of GDP, has been coming down continuously and we say we will work to better the predictions for 2029. The only indicator that is moving away from the original forecast is the interest cost, which is a little high. But, if you look at exchange rate stability, the inflation interest rates, the current account surplus and the official reserves, we have beaten the targets that were given to us. So, that is why we keep on asking anyone to tell us an indicator which indicates that Sri Lanka would derail again, that Sri Lanka would fail again. These are the fears you all have in your minds, you all dream in your dreams for this Government to fail and for you all to come to power. That is not going to happen, we would say."
  },
