    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "ae7918e0b646ca42cd6c428764223a7b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1916,
    "chunk_text": "Sir, various quarters in this House has accorded sentiments, but one has to realize that casino and gambling have already been legitimized and that what needs to be debated before this House is whether the present Gambling Regulatory Authority Bill has oversight, accountability and focuses on responsible governance.\n\nSir, if we speak about gambling, in pre-colonial times also there were various forms of gambling like \"cheetu\", cock-fighting et cetera. I would not delve in to that; I think the Hon. (Dr.) (Ms.) Kaushalya dealt with it quiet extensively. The first regulatory framework was brought during colonial times, the Gaming Ordinance, No. 17 of 1889. That criminalized unlawful gambling and common place gambling. Further, horse racing is also a form of gambling, which was regulated under Betting on Horse-Racing Ordinance.\n\nDuring post-Independence era, Sri Lanka took a different stand. Gambling was not encouraged; it was somewhat prohibited. However, the State, through various lotteries like the Hospital Lottery in the 1950s and the National Lotteries Board, encouraged gambling. A sweep is a form of gambling. So, on the one hand, the State said, \"Do not gamble\" and on the other, it realized that it is necessary and used the revenue from gambling for development. The open economy in 1977 saw the emergence of the casino industry. It was an illegal industry, but the governments turned a blind eye to that. Then, they brought in the Betting and Gaming Levy Act, No. 40 of 1988. On the one side, the Government said gaming and casino are illegal, but it was used as a revenue-generating exercise. So, the Government, while saying it was an illegal act, taxed gaming, thereby giving it tacit acceptance. The Government did not have the courage to say that it is legitimate and said it was illegal, maybe, because of politics or other social norms, but thought fit to collect revenue through taxes."
  },
