    "md5": "29e6738f0900e2e663a884682f815381",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1966,
    "chunk_text": "During post-Independence era, Sri Lanka took a different stand. Gambling was not encouraged; it was somewhat prohibited. However, the State, through various lotteries like the Hospital Lottery in the 1950s and the National Lotteries Board, encouraged gambling. A sweep is a form of gambling. So, on the one hand, the State said, \"Do not gamble\" and on the other, it realized that it is necessary and used the revenue from gambling for development. The open economy in 1977 saw the emergence of the casino industry. It was an illegal industry, but the governments turned a blind eye to that. Then, they brought in the Betting and Gaming Levy Act, No. 40 of 1988. On the one side, the Government said gaming and casino are illegal, but it was used as a revenue-generating exercise. So, the Government, while saying it was an illegal act, taxed gaming, thereby giving it tacit acceptance. The Government did not have the courage to say that it is legitimate and said it was illegal, maybe, because of politics or other social norms, but thought fit to collect revenue through taxes.\n\n1937 1938\n\n\u0db4\u0dcf\u0dbb\u0dca\u0dbd\u0dd2\u0d9c\u0db8\u0dca\u0db1\u0dca\u0dad\u0dd4\u0dc0\n\nbeen a revenue loss of approximately US Dollars 1 billion for the country. In 2015, the then Government cancelled all the permits and all the concessions given and with that, Mr. Packer left. And, that was the end of their project.\n\nAt present, casino operators are not given any form of concession in terms of taxes and levies. In addition to the local operators, there is a foreign operator who has invested US Dollars 125 million and that, too, is subjected to the same taxation and levies. So, there is a level playing field and through taxation, the State is able to obtain the much needed revenue. However, when we look at the 2010 Act, there were various issues and grievances. They said that there is no proper mechanism to monitor money laundering, and gaming was somewhat unregulated because of the loopholes in the law."
