    "chunk_index": 111,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "cba373862d22911717b3e4ae1adbf314",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1369,
    "chunk_text": "At present, casino operators are not given any form of concession in terms of taxes and levies. In addition to the local operators, there is a foreign operator who has invested US Dollars 125 million and that, too, is subjected to the same taxation and levies. So, there is a level playing field and through taxation, the State is able to obtain the much needed revenue. However, when we look at the 2010 Act, there were various issues and grievances. They said that there is no proper mechanism to monitor money laundering, and gaming was somewhat unregulated because of the loopholes in the law.\n\nThe present Bill replaces certain old enactments that were in existence. In addition to the land-based casino industry, there is a global online casino industry, which is valued at US Dollars 110 billion. Anybody who has a smartphone can access online gaming. So, I appeal to the Government to bring in more stringent laws and regulations. Casinos do not attract the common man; it affects the common man less. However, online gaming could be an addiction. Therefore, I appeal to this Government to look at bringing in very stringent laws and regulations to monitor online gaming. Further, the physical casino industry creates jobs and entice tourism, but if we have online gaming, its revenue will go out of the country and there would be no net benefit to the country."
  },
  {
