    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "5b836a4aae8a005629c6ec259632bbae",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1951,
    "chunk_text": "The present Bill replaces certain old enactments that were in existence. In addition to the land-based casino industry, there is a global online casino industry, which is valued at US Dollars 110 billion. Anybody who has a smartphone can access online gaming. So, I appeal to the Government to bring in more stringent laws and regulations. Casinos do not attract the common man; it affects the common man less. However, online gaming could be an addiction. Therefore, I appeal to this Government to look at bringing in very stringent laws and regulations to monitor online gaming. Further, the physical casino industry creates jobs and entice tourism, but if we have online gaming, its revenue will go out of the country and there would be no net benefit to the country.\n\nIn addition, Sri Lanka encourages casinos in this country only for the purpose of enticing foreigners to come and gamble here. Therefore, I would urge the\n\nGovernment to consider having a representative from the tourism sector represented in the Authority, thereby issues in relation to tourism could be further addressed.\n\nAlso, Sir, I believe there is ambiguity in this Bill with regard to certain provisions. Clause 22 refers to a main cash desk. Today, we are in a digital age. A main cash desk should not be interpreted as a physical main cash desk, but should include other generally accepted forms of payment like electronic transactions, which would connect to one centralized system. So, the definition of \u201cmain cash desk\u201d should mean one centralized system.\n\nThe term \u201ccurrency\u201d in Clause 22, I believe, should be read with Clause 59 to include electronic fund transfers because if you give a very restrictive meaning, especially in this day and age where everything is in a digital form and everything is electronic, it would create issues. So, allowing other forms of payment would be better for attracting overseas travellers and would also remain auditable."
  },
