    "md5": "6841f090c663762cd3a1fc655b94d728",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1551,
    "chunk_text": "What are our international monetary obligations? Sri Lanka is a current-account convertible economy under Article VIII of the International Monetary Fund. Simply put, a country that accepts Article VIII must not impose restrictions on payments and transfers for current international transactions, except with IMF approval. Gambling-related payments are current-account services under the IMF\u2019s Balance of Payments Manual. The operator\u2019s margin is recorded as a service charge, while wins and losses among players are current transfers. So, we must enable such payments, but through formal, supervised financial channels and not through other illegal channels.\n\n1989 1990\n\n\u0db4\u0dcf\u0dbb\u0dca\u0dbd\u0dd2\u0d9c\u0db8\u0dca\u0db1\u0dca\u0dad\u0dd4\u0dc0\n\nThe Bill is silent on junkets and VIP promoters, the entities which historically bridged gamblers and underground banking. The international experience in Macau and other areas shows that unregulated junkets are prime conduits for illicit cross-border flows. Our Bill has no licensing class, no fit and proper joint liability and no ban on promoter of deposit-taking. So, that is another violation.\n\nThe other is, at our borders, the practical regime permits any amount to be brought or taken with declarations required at US Dollars 15,000 of total foreign exchange and US Dollars 10,000 in currency notes. Declarations alone are not a control on downstream use. Without a bank-rail mandate inside casinos, this becomes the perfect pretext for cash-based foreign exchange recycling, rent-seeking and corruption risks in the cage."
  },
