    "chunk_text": "4. Market diversification and trade agreements: How many new trade agreements or preferential access arrangements would be finalized by 2030? Why are previously signed agreements not operational yet and what new countries or regions are being targeted? Is Sri Lanka considering joining any regional trade groupings?\n\nHon. Speaker, the Free Trade Agreements with Singapore and Thailand had been signed, sealed and delivered, but are not put into operation. What particular problem is causing delay?\n\nHon. Deputy Minister, as you know, our interest rates are between 13 per cent to 20 per cent, while our peers in th region are having rates between 4 per cent to 10 per cent.\n\n8. Investment mobilization: What levels of local and foreign direct investments are projected between now and 2030? Through which frameworks - BoI Law, Port City Law or Companies Act - are these investments being mobilized?\n\n2251 2252\n\n\u0db4\u0dcf\u0dbb\u0dca\u0dd2\u0d9c\u0db8\u0dca\u0db1\u0dca\u0dd4\u0dc0\n\n9. Incentives for investors: What specific tax incentives, tax holidays, qualifying investment expenditure allowances and concessional lending schemes are being offered to attract investments into export-oriented sectors? 10. Debt and reserve management: Given that Sri Lanka must resume large-scale debt repayments from April, 2028, could the Government\u2019s export and investment drive help build sufficient foreign reserves in advance? The Public Debt Management Act ensures exactly that. So, that is the answer I am seeking for.\n\nThe Public Debt Management Act ensures exactly that. So, that is the answer I am seeking for.\n\nFurther, can the Minister state:\n\n(i) the level of foreign reserves as at 01st January, 2025; and (ii) the month-by-month accumulation of reserves by the Central Bank from January, 2025 to date?\n\nThank you, Sir."
  },
