    "md5": "137dbebfba2451c8a9ae69bda5a1b31d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1857,
    "chunk_text": "Further, the recovery actions are mainly governed by the terms and conditions agreed to between licensed banks, finance companies or specialized leasing companies and borrowers through finance leasing and loan agreements.\n\n3. Directions are issued in line with the best international practices and to ensure adequate risk management procedures are followed within the sector. Demarcation between willful defaulters and involuntary defaulters is difficult at the regulatory level. However, LBs, FCs, and SLCs are advised to engage in negotiations with borrowers in good faith on a case-by-case basis.\n\n4. The method currently in place is to review the hardships caused by events outside the borrowers\u2019 control and provide appropriate relief. For example, at the time of the Easter Sunday attack, the COVID-19 pandemic and the subsequent economic crisis, a debt moratorium scheme was introduced with several concessions including temporary grace periods and established Business Revival Units and Post-COVID Revival Units with the objective of facilitating sustainable revival of viable businesses affected by those extraordinary macroeconomic conditions and financial hardships resulted from the pandemic.\n\n5. Borrowers are able to seek redress through the Mediation Boards or the Financial Ombudsman. Further, a customer who is subject to unfair treatment can lodge a complaint to the Financial Consumer Relations Department of the Central Bank of Sri Lanka. The Central Bank of Sri Lanka may call for additional information/documents, for a hearing from all/any party involved and/or conduct a spot examination/inquiry, as applicable. Based on the determination, the Central Bank of Sri Lanka may require LBs/FCs/SLCs to take specific actions where necessary. Also, borrowers have the option to take necessary legal action under contract or consumer law."
