    "chunk_index": 154,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "16081b7f03b5db19da039f90ec0999c6",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1793,
    "chunk_text": "Again, may I refer to another Italian philosopher, Hannah Arendt, who had written widely on these issues and this is something she had said, I quote:\n\n\u201cThe sad truth is that most evil is done by people who never make up their minds to be good or evil.\u201d\n\nAnd, having listen to all the Hon. Members of the Opposition who have spoken with all humility, I would like to place on record that a different kind of culture of good practices is slowly - I grant you slowly - but gradually emerging. I think acknowledging this is extremely important. What we are seeing today is the space for people to act based on a conscience, based on an engagement with their own conscience and based on an understanding of what is good and what is evil. We, as a Government, firmly believe that in each human being, there is goodness and a desire for goodness. What we are seeing today is the ability for that goodness to flourish, for that goodness to emerge, for that conscience to be stirred and for their actions to be guided by that conscience and by that goodness, which is inherently\n\nthere in all of us. The ability to highlight their empathy, to be able to act with empathy, to be able to understand and relate to someone else\u2019s experiences - the suffering, emotions, feelings of someone - and to respond accordingly is what we are seeing emerging slowly in our country. So, I think, with all humility, we must acknowledge that that is beginning to happen, that this stirring of our conscience, the flourishing of goodness, the expectation that people would act with the goodness within all of them is happening, that that space is slowly emerging. Therefore, I think we need to acknowledge that; I think it is important that we acknowledge that because this is how change and transformation begins."
  },
