    "lang": "si-ta-en",
    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/documents/hansard/1756894696039492.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2025-08-22-2025-08-22-0215",
    "chunk_index": 215,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "330fea9e687c9843340cf11ea9dbc2b7",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1553,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cThe Government has pledged a fresh direction on several long-standing issues, including addressing some emblematic cases to deliver justice to victims, restoring the rule of law, eliminating ethnic discrimination and divisive politics.\u201d\n\nAnd, also with regard to the rule of law, I can see many steps being taken and I am pretty sure that it shows that the Judiciary of this country is working in an independent manner. Certain high profile cases have been taken up and many former Ministers were arrested. With regard to persons who had been at higher positions than that, at the presidential level, certain actions are taking place. So, things are moving. Maybe, things are moving at a little slower speed. However, we have the urge to change things and it is happening.\n\nThe Minister of Foreign Affairs, my Minister, made a historic speech today. He also emphasized the need to repeal the PTA and especially mentioned that by early September, a repeal Bill would be presented to Parliament, which, we feel, would be one of the major turning points. I, as a Member of the JVP, know that the PTA was brought in during the 70s\u2019 and our party was one of the key victims of the PTA. If that was brought in for oppression, we believe that, now, we have the chance to repeal that and replace it with something which is not oppressive. But, if we need to address certain issues like underworld activities or some drug-related crimes, we would introduced some legislation on par with the global judicial system, but that would not be an oppressive law."
  },
